From b6b56e372faa1fe00bfb4ff7e5933805845927d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 03:37:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm-lease: let desktop monitors be leased, and send released

Only connectors the kernel marks non-desktop (VR headsets) were ever offered
through wp_drm_lease_device_v1, and those Hyprland never drives. A new monitor
rule key, leasable, offers a normal monitor the same way:

    hl.monitor({ output = "DP-2", mode = "preferred", leasable = true })

The monitor is used as usual until a client leases it. The kernel does not stop
a lessor from committing to objects it has leased out, so Hyprland has to get
out of the way itself:

- Before the lease is created the monitor is released like a disconnect
  (CMonitor::releaseForLease): workspaces and focus move to another monitor,
  the frame scheduler and any queued async commit are dropped, and the output
  is committed disabled. That commit is a blocking modeset through
  CDRMOutput::commit(), which also cancels aquamarine's pending async commit
  and pauses the CRTC's commit queue, so our last flip has completed and the
  CRTC and planes are off before the lessee gets them. The lease is refused
  (finished, monitor restored) if aquamarine still reports the output enabled.
  The disable commit leaves connector->crtc assigned, which is what
  CDRMLease::create builds the lease from.
- While leased every commit path is shut: CMonitorState::commit/test refuse,
  applyMonitorRule and onConnect return early, rule reloads skip the monitor
  (only the offer follows the rule), and aquamarine's state requests are
  ignored. The output's mode is cleared so aquamarine's restoreAfterVT does
  not re-commit it after a VT switch.
- When the lease ends (client destroys it, the lessee closes its fd and
  aquamarine's scanLeases sees it gone, the monitor is unplugged) the monitor
  is reconnected from an idle callback with its current rule, a full modeset
  as on hotplug.

A monitor that is both disabled and leasable is offered but never driven,
for outputs meant only for the lessee. Rule changes to leasable take the soft
path, so toggling it withdraws or offers the connector without a modeset.

Protocol fixes on the way, all of which affect VR leases too:

- release now sends the released destructor event the protocol requires;
  clients wait for it before cleaning up.
- A lease names monitors, not connector objects, so a client destroying its
  connector object no longer strands the monitor as "being leased" forever.
- finished is sent at most once. aquamarine emits destroy twice for one lease
  (on revoke or lessee exit, and again when freed), and the second one used to
  send finished again, including into a lease object the client had just
  destroyed.
- Withdrawn connectors are refused with finished, as the protocol says.
- A leased monitor that is unplugged gets its lease revoked.
- When a lease ends, clients that destroyed their connector object get the
  connector advertised again.
- The lease resource holds its protocol directly, so destroying a lease still
  revokes it after the client released the device object.
- Connectors advertised when a client binds the device are made after the
  device has its weak self pointer, so they know their device: done follows
  withdrawn for them, and destroying one removes it. Before, every client that
  bound the device left its connector objects in the compositor for good.
- Requests and connectors, like leases, hold the protocol directly: a
  disconnecting client's device object is destroyed before them (libwayland
  goes by id), and one released first no longer strands them either.
- A connector must come from the device object the request was made on
  (wrong_device), so one lease cannot name a monitor twice through two
  bindings of the global; a monitor named twice is refused before anything
  is released.

Who may lease a monitor, how often, and taking it back. With only
leasable, any client that sees the lease device may lease the monitor, as
often as it likes, until it lets go. Two more rule keys and a dispatcher
narrow that; all are off by default, which is the behaviour above:

- lessee: a user name or a uid. Only clients whose connection's uid (the
  SO_PEERCRED libwayland keeps) is that user's are sent the monitor's
  connector, and a submit is checked again against the rule in force then.
  A name that does not resolve, or a number that is not a uid, leases to
  nobody, and is a config error. Hyprland's permission rules cannot do
  this for a sandboxed lessee: they match /proc/<pid>/exe, which the
  compositor cannot read for an undumpable process or one of another uid.
- lease_interval: milliseconds, at most an hour. A request sooner than
  that after the monitor was last released for a lease (granted or not) is
  answered finished before anything is released, so asking in a loop costs
  one release per interval instead of one per request.
- hl.dsp.revoke_lease({ monitor = ... }), or every lease without a
  monitor: terminate() the lease (the kernel revokes it) and tell the client
  (finished). The monitor stays offered; withdraw it first (leasable =
  false) to keep it.

The offer and its lessee follow the rule on every rule check, even when the
rest of the rule cannot be applied or the monitor is leased, so a narrowed
lessee takes effect at once. A lease already granted stays until it ends or
is revoked.

When a lease ends, for any reason, the monitor is now first committed off
with no buffer, a blocking modeset, before it is reconnected: that waits for
a flip the lessee still had queued, and takes the lessee's framebuffers off
the primary and the cursor plane. The reconnect's modeset replaced only the
primary's; aquamarine touches the cursor plane only when the cursor
changes, so a lessee's cursor image could stay on the desktop.

hyprctl monitors reports leasable, leased, lessee (a uid, any or nobody)
and leaseInterval.

Aquamarine still reassigns a disabled connector's CRTC on hotplug rechecks
(recheckCRTCs), which can hand a leased CRTC to another connector; that needs
a small aquamarine change carried separately.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
